<h2>A land deal built on family settlements</h2>
<p>The agreement of 17 January 2022 was unusual in a way that proved decisive: clauses 6 and 7 tied the conveyance of parts of the Gadaipur land to family settlements within the seller&rsquo;s family, with 31 March 2022 fixed as the cut-off under clause 6. Sale deeds for part of the land were registered in July and August 2022; a deed of exchange with a family member followed in 2024; demand letters and a termination notice of December 2024 brought the relationship to an end. The High Court had appointed a sole arbitrator in January 2025, and the award of 17 July 2026 refused specific performance, directing instead that the seller refund Rs 97 lakh with 15% interest and pay Rs 25 lakh in costs.</p>

<h2>The buyer&rsquo;s challenge, and its limits</h2>
<p>The buyer attacked the award on multiple fronts: that the arbitrator refused to decide framed issues on the price, misread clauses 6 and 7, and wrongly discounted his proof of readiness and willingness. The Court&rsquo;s starting point was the standard of review. Under <em>MMTC v. Vedanta</em>, (2019) 4 SCC 163, and the Supreme Court&rsquo;s recent restatement in <em>OPG Power Generation</em>, (2025) 2 SCC 417, a Section 34 court does not re-appreciate evidence or substitute its own view; patent illegality must go to the root of the matter and cannot be a dressed-up appeal on facts.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The price issue.</strong> The complaint that the arbitrator ignored framed issues failed: the award had addressed the consideration by comparing rates in the executed sale deeds, and no competing evidence displaced the Rs 22 crore figure.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The clause 6 and 7 architecture.</strong> The arbitrator read the clauses together, holding the 31 March 2022 cut-off operative, with post-deadline payments referable to the clause 6 land — a plausible construction of the contract, which is the tribunal&rsquo;s domain.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Readiness and willingness.</strong> The finding that the buyer failed to prove continuous readiness under Section 16(c) of the Specific Relief Act — with CA certificates held insufficient — was a factual assessment beyond Section 34&rsquo;s reach.</div>
</div>
<p>Finding no ground under Section 34(2) or 34(2A), the Court upheld the award in full, including the refund, interest and costs directions, and disposed of the companion Section 9 petition as infructuous.</p>

<h2>The larger signal for property arbitrations</h2>
<p>Specific performance disputes over Delhi-area land increasingly travel through arbitration, and this judgment illustrates both halves of what that choice means. The tribunal has full power to refuse specific relief and mould a monetary remedy — refund with meaningful interest and substantial costs, as here. And the losing party&rsquo;s recourse is genuinely narrow: unless the award ignores the contract&rsquo;s terms altogether or offends a fundamental policy of Indian law, the challenge will fail. Buyers who intend to seek specific performance should treat the proof of readiness and willingness — bank statements, sanctioned funding, consistent conduct — as the evidentiary spine of their case before the arbitrator, because no court will rebuild it afterwards.</p>
<div class="note">Section 34 petitions must be filed within three months (extendable by thirty days) of receipt of the award, and lie to the court having jurisdiction over the seat. The 2018 amendment to the Specific Relief Act made specific performance the general rule rather than the exception for contracts after its commencement, but the requirement of readiness and willingness under Section 16(c) remains. This article is informational only.</div>
